  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Bell Textron is a major federal contractor with $1.9B in obligations for FY2026 to date. Bell Textron earned that total across 696 awards, with an average award size of $2.8M, and obligations are up 26.5% year over year. Bell Textron received steadily larger obligations across the last six fiscal years, moving from $349M in FY21 to $307M in FY22, $985M in FY23, $1.2B in FY24, $1.5B in FY25, and $1.9B in FY26. Bell Textron’s business is concentrated with the Department of Defense, which obligated $1.9B for FY2026 to date. Bell Textron’s product and service mix is focused on aircraft-related markets: Aircraft Manufacturing accounted for $1.8B, and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing accounted for $164M. Bell Textron also reports smaller obligations in consulting, engineering, and training markets, including Other Scientific and Technical Consulting Services at $761K and Engineering Services at $635K. This profile shows where federal buyers are placing funds and which NAICS categories captured the largest dollar flows.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Bell Textron’s federal obligations by agency?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, with $1.9B in obligations for FY2026 to date. The Department of the Interior follows at $53.2M. Other agencies recorded much smaller obligations: the Department of Justice at $278K, the Department of Transportation at $135K, and the Department of Energy at $58.5K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets capture most of Bell Textron’s federal revenue?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest market, with $1.8B in obligations for FY2026 to date. Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing captured $164M. Bell Textron also has obligations in Scale and Balance Manufacturing at $1.4M, Other Scientific and Technical Consulting Services at $761K, and Engineering Services at $635K."
      }
    ]
  },
